    "title": "Entire Tulare County town under evacuation warning after wildfire ignites - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Miranda Adams ,\n5 hours ago\nTULARE COUNTY, Calif. ( KSEE/KGPE ) – An entire Tulare County town is under an evacuation warning after a wildfire broke out Wednesday afternoon.\nThe Alpaugh Fire ignited around 2:40 p.m., near Avenue 42 and Road 38, just south of Alpaugh.\nBy 3:40 p.m., it had grown to 500 acres, spurring evacuation warnings in zones TLC-E298 and TLC-E275, which include about half the community of Alpaugh and the entire town of Allensworth.\nBy 7:30 p.m., it had exploded in size to 3,600 acres, making it one of the largest active wildfires in the state.\nThe fire department asks that residents be prepared by packing essentials, creating arrangements for their pets, and helping neighbors.\nThe cause of the fire is under investigation.\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed.\nFor the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to YourCentralValley.com | CBS47 and KSEE24.",
    "summary": "TULARE COUNTY, Calif. ( KSEE/KGPE ) – An entire Tulare County town is under an evacuation warning after a wildfire broke out Wednesday afternoon. By 3:40 p.m., it had grown to 500 acres, spurring evacuation warnings in zones TLC-E298 and TLC-E275, which include about half the community of Alpaugh and the entire town of Allensworth. By 7:30 p.m., it had exploded in size to 3,600 acres, making it one of the largest active wildfires in the state. The fire department asks that residents be prepared by packing essentials, creating arrangements for their pets, and helping neighbors.",
